I can absolutely see someone launching Hoefax as the male retaliatory mirror of “Are We Dating the Same Guy?” The name practically writes the pitch itself: Carfax, but for dating history.

And it would probably become toxic almost immediately. Crowdsourced sexual-history claims combine unverifiable testimony, revenge incentives, mistaken identity, privacy violations, screenshots detached from context, and permanent reputational damage. Sexual history is also much harder to establish objectively than whether a car had an accident.

Drago’s punch: symmetry does not create fairness. If one side builds a dangerous surveillance weapon, giving the other side the matching weapon produces an arms race—not justice.

A legitimate version would need to track verifiable safety-relevant conduct, not body counts or humiliation:

* confirmed violence, stalking, fraud, or coercion;
* identity verification and strong appeal rights;
* evidence thresholds;
* expiration and correction mechanisms;
* no intimate images or doxxing;
* gender-neutral access and standards.

The underlying demand is real: people want protection from deception and dangerous partners. But “sexual credit score” would rapidly turn vulnerability into ammunition. The better invention is a consent-and-safety system, not a gendered shame ledger.